Make LÖVE build for visionOS, render without a presentable layer, boot from a
host that owns @main and its main thread, and adopt textures it did not create.

Part 1 -- building at all. visionOS defines TARGET_OS_IPHONE, so config.h takes
the LOVE_IOS branch and the iOS backend compiles, which is what we want: Metal,
the iOS filesystem layout, the SDL3 UIKit path. Three things stop it:

  1. common/ios.mm uses UIScreen in showGameList; visionOS has none, because
     there is no single "main screen" to take bounds from. That function is
     LÖVE's picker for several .love files and is unreachable for a bundle with
     one fused game.love, so it only has to compile.
  2. graphics/Graphics.cpp calls opengl::createInstance() unconditionally while
     the Vulkan and Metal backends beside it are already guarded. Every GLES
     symbol in XROS.sdk is API_UNAVAILABLE(visionos).
  3. Hence a matching LOVE_GRAPHICS_OPENGL macro in config.h, defined
     everywhere except visionOS. Worth upstreaming on its own: it makes "a
     platform with no OpenGL backend" expressible, which is a gap today.

Part 2 -- rendering with nowhere to present. A fully immersive visionOS app has
no CAMetalLayer; frames go to CompositorServices drawables and the flat 2D
image becomes a texture the host shows in a window or in-world.
metal::Graphics::setMode accepts a null context and stands an offscreen texture
in for the drawable. Render passes, MSAA resolve and screenshot readback keep
working against it; only presentDrawable is skipped. MTLStorageModeShared so
the host can read it back without a staging blit -- free on unified memory.

Part 3 -- booting from a host that owns @main AND the main thread.
love_visionos_boot() runs LÖVE without a main(); LOVE_NO_MAIN suppresses LÖVE's
main() and SDL_main.h's takeover with it (SDL_MAIN_HANDLED), which otherwise
leaves an undefined _SDL_main.

Window::setWindow gets a headless path that creates NO SDL window at all. This
is not an optimisation. SDL's UIKit backend touches UIApplication during both
video init and window creation, and BoardServices asserts a main-run-loop
barrier at each -- so with LÖVE on a thread of its own, which it must be for
the host to keep its main thread for SwiftUI and CompositorServices, any of it
traps. love_visionos_prepareOnMainThread() brings SDL's video and event
subsystems up on the main thread first, so LÖVE's own SDL_InitSubSystem is a
refcount bump that touches nothing.

All of it is gated on love_visionos_getHeadless(), a runtime flag rather than
an #ifdef, so the flat visionOS build keeps presenting through SDL's layer
exactly as on iOS.

Part 4 -- adopting textures LÖVE did not create. metal::Texture gains a
constructor taking an existing id<MTLTexture>, and metal::Graphics a
newTextureFromHandle() that wraps one as a render target, so a renderer can
draw straight into CompositorServices drawables instead of into its own
canvases followed by a copy.

No ownership flag: under ARC, assigning retains and the destructor's
texture = nil releases only our reference. No MSAA companion: a drawable
arrives resolved, and a private MSAA texture for one we do not own would
double memory the compositor already budgeted. No updateGraphicsMemorySize():
counting a drawable would report memory this process never allocated. The
Metal -> LÖVE format mapping throws on anything unrecognised rather than
guessing, because a wrong guess is silently wrong colour.


Part 5 -- reporting the save directory. love_visionos_saveDirectory() asks the
filesystem module where LOVE actually writes. Guessing that from platform
convention is how a ROM ends up in a directory the engine never looks at,
which is exactly what happened.


Part 6 -- love.xr. A plain table of functions (not a love Module: that would
mean editing Module::ModuleType, a header everything includes, for no gain)
exposing the CompositorServices frame loop and ARKit device anchor to Lua:
beginFrame, views, eyeCanvas, submitFrame.

Lua drives the loop. beginFrame() blocks until the compositor is ready and
returns the pose for the frame being submitted; the alternative -- a host loop
calling into Lua -- would render with whatever pose Lua last saw, already
stale by submission. The per-eye field of view is handed over in OpenXR's
signed convention, because that is what the voxel mod's VRRig and
Mat4.fovProjection already consume.

Part 7 -- one command queue, not two. love_visionos_commandQueue() hands the
shell the queue LOVE submits on, and everything that samples the virtual
screen encodes onto it: the flat launcher window and the panel the immersive
renderer maps the frame onto.

Both were using queues of their own. Metal keeps command buffers in commit
order WITHIN a queue and gives no ordering at all BETWEEN queues, so a reader
could encode its sample into the gap between LOVE's clear and LOVE's draws and
come away with an empty frame -- intermittently, which looked like flicker and
was reported as such from both presentations.

Sharing the queue removes the race instead of narrowing it, at the price of
not overlapping a reader's frame with LOVE's on the GPU. The alternative is a
rotating set of snapshot textures plus an MTLSharedEvent to order them, which
is the right answer if that serialisation ever shows up in a frame time; at
these resolutions it should not.

